Is == bad? When should it and should it not be used? What's the difference?

[Response A]
The "==" operator is an equality comparison that checks if two objects have equal values or references. It returns true only when both sides point to the same object (i.e., they refer to the exact same memory location). 

On the other hand, ".equals()" method compares the content of two objects by comparing their contents as well as any additional properties such as hash codes or string representations. If these differences match between the two objects, then ".equals()" will return true; otherwise false.

In general, use "==" for simple comparisons where you know exactly what type each variable represents. For example:

```java
if ("hello".equals("Hello")) {
    // Do something!
}
```

However, if your variables represent different types, like this:

```java
String str1 = new String("hello");
int num1 = 5;

// This won't work because int cannot be converted to String
if(str1.equals(num1)) { 
    System.out.println("Strings are equal!");
} else {
    System.out.println("Strings aren't equal.");
}

System.out.println(str1); // prints: hello

num2 = 6;
if(str1.equals(num2)) { 
    System.out.println("Integers are equal!");
} else {
    System.out.println("Integers aren't equal.");
}
```

Here we're trying to check whether `str1` equals `num2`, but since integers can also hold Strings, `.equals()` would fail here. So we need to explicitly cast either side to a String before calling `.equals()`.

[Response B]
The "==" (equality) operator is an overloaded method that compares two objects for equality based on their value or reference type. It returns true if both operands have the same memory address and false otherwise.

On the other hand, ".equals()" (case-insensitive comparison with case-sensitive substring matching) checks whether two strings contain exactly the same characters at every position they share. If there are any differences between the two strings, then ".equals()" will return false.

In general, when comparing strings in Java, you can use either "==" or ".equals()", but always prefer using ".equals()" because it provides more accurate results even though its implementation may differ slightly from the standard library version.

Here’s how you would write your code:

```java
