public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
    // If n is less than or equal to zero, then it cannot be a power of three (except for 1)
    if(n < 1){
        return false;
    }
    
    while(true){
        int remainder = n % 3;
        
        if(remainder != 0 && remainder != 1){
            break; // We've found a non-zero remainder and not a single digit number
        } else{
            n /= 3; // Divide by 3 until we get back to our original value
        }
    }
    
    return n == 1; // Return true only when n becomes 1 after division
}
```

This function works as follows:

1. First check whether `n` is negative since powers of three can't have negative exponents. In this case, they're all positive numbers greater than zero.
2. Check whether `n` itself is already a power of three using modulo operation (`%`). This will give us either 0 or 1 depending on whether `n` has any factors other than 1 and itself.
3. If both conditions fail, divide `n` by 3 repeatedly until we reach a result of 1. At each step, remove the last digit from `n`.
4. Finally, compare the resulting `n` with 1. If they match, then `n` was indeed a power of three, so return `true`. Otherwise, return `false`.

Note that this algorithm assumes that `n` is within the range `[2, 8e9]`, which should cover most practical cases. However, larger values may require more memory and computation time. Also note that some languages might optimize away redundant calculations like checking divisibility against 3 before dividing by 3. Therefore, consider testing your code across different platforms and environments.


